  "query_preview": "..\nDry weather will continue across the area through the remainder\nof month, and into early November. Surface high pressure and\nupper level ridging look to remain the dominant features across\nthe area for the next several days. A dry frontal boundary\nlooks to pass through the area overnight tonight, which will\ntransition winds to be out of the northwest on Friday. This will\ncause downsloping across the Piedmont, which will lead to well\nabove average temperatures across the area for Friday. \n\nCloudy conditions moving over the area from an upstream system\nwill lead to much cooler temperatures on Saturday for Halloween\nand Trick or Treating festivities. \n\nAll models have officially backed off on any widespread\nprecipitation chances for the region on Sunday/Monday of this\ncoming week. The fetch of moisture that was initially forecast\nby models earlier in the week has been choked off by another\nsurface high pressure system that is expected to park itself\nover the Northeast on Sunday and Monday. This system on Sunday\nand Monday as it approaches the area never really looks to tap\ninto the Gulf or Atlantic moisture, which is evident in looking\nat IVT analysis that keep moisture transport well to our south\nand to our northwest. While precipitation chances have\npredominantly faded to only a slight chance for showers across\nupslope regions for this system, significant cloud cover is\nexpected areawide, which should keep temperatures near average\nfor this time of the year even with an upper level ridge\nremaining over the Southeast. \n\nAll eyes turn towards the middle to late week system, which\nensemble data is in much better agreement of an amplified upper\nlevel trough digging south into the Gulf states before lifting\nnortheast through the Mid-Atlantic states. There also appears to\nbe strong support for a much more favorable environment for \nmoisture to be pulled north ahead of this aforementioned upper\nlevel trough into the region from the Gulf and Atlantic. While \nit is still too soon to discuss specifics of this system, it \nwill likely be the next system to provide any meaningful \nwidespread precipitation to the region. Timing and specifics on\nthis system are still in question.",
